No celebration today for the Nigerian 
pupils abducted in May. The bandit leader
lines them up to march into the forest—
just like I did on Children's Day.

They march on to the bugles and whistles 
of market women circling the bonfire
of a thief's body. Left, right. Left, right.

Marching down the road with potholes,
they halt to salute the roadside disco
of red-blue lights and siren-music,
as thieves pass in government vehicles.


Left, right. Left, right.
They march over breathless bodies 
lying still for the national anthem. 
Move discreetly through streets 

where soldiers circle a Biafran boy, 
like deliverance ministers
preparing to cast out his spirit. 

They go past streets with no electricity, 
and a house with a petrol generator 
stands out like a red carpet for darkness.

They march on with black boots 
past the mosque of a radical cleric, 
where the muezzin dyes the long grey hair
of the afternoon sky into darkness.

They march and march into the polling booth 
where we will stand in line next election. 
Their fathers cast their bodies into the ballot box.
What are they expecting—a girl or a boy?
   



